window.__hvBdRegister("metaphysics", {"key":"metaphysics","sections":[{"i":0,"j":13,"loc":"Book I, part 1","title":"Wisdom as knowledge of first causes","summary":"Aristotle opens with the claim that all men by nature desire to know, and traces a ladder from sensation and memory to experience, art and science. Experience knows that something is so, art knows why, so the masterworker who knows causes is wiser than the manual worker. Drawing on common notions of the wise man, he concludes that Wisdom is the most universal, exact and authoritative science, the science of first principles and causes, including the good as end. Since philosophy began in wonder and is pursued for its own sake, it is the only free science and the most divine; it ends in the opposite of wonder, as the geometer is untroubled by the incommensurable diagonal.","ideas":["Sensation, memory, experience, art and science form an ascending order of knowing","Knowledge of causes, not mere practical success, marks the wiser person","Wisdom is a theoretical science of first principles, sought for its own sake","Philosophy begins in wonder and ends when the cause is grasped"],"terms":[{"t":"sophia (Wisdom)","d":"The science of first principles and causes, most universal and most authoritative."},{"t":"empeiria (experience)","d":"Knowledge of individual cases built from many memories, without knowing the cause."},{"t":"technē (art)","d":"A universal judgement about a class of cases that knows the reason why."}]},{"i":14,"j":32,"loc":"Book I, part 2","title":"Early thinkers on matter and motion","summary":"Aristotle names the four causes from his work on nature (essence, matter, source of change, end) and reviews his predecessors to test them. Most early philosophers recognised only a material principle: Thales water, Anaximenes and Diogenes air, Hippasus and Heraclitus fire, Empedocles four elements, Anaxagoras infinitely many. The facts forced others to seek a source of movement; Anaxagoras's reason seemed sober beside earlier random talk, and Hesiod, Parmenides and Empedocles posited love, or friendship and strife. Aristotle judges that they grasped matter and moving cause only vaguely, like untrained fighters, and closes with the atomists Leucippus and Democritus, who neglected movement.","ideas":["Four causes: essence, matter, source of movement, end","The first philosophers recognised only material principles","Reason, love, and friendship and strife introduced a moving cause","Atoms differ only by shape, order and position"],"terms":[{"t":"archē (principle)","d":"The first thing from which something is, comes to be, or is known."},{"t":"hupokeimenon (substratum)","d":"What underlies change and persists through it, as the early thinkers' matter did."}]},{"i":33,"j":49,"loc":"Book I, part 3","title":"Pythagoreans, Eleatics and Plato on principles","summary":"The Pythagoreans, raised in mathematics, took the elements of number (odd and even, limited and unlimited) to be the elements of all things, and some listed ten pairs of contraries; Alcmaeon spoke of contraries loosely. Parmenides, Melissus and Xenophanes held the universe one; only Parmenides is credited with some insight, positing hot and cold. Plato, shaped by Heraclitean flux and Socrates' search for definitions, placed definitions in separate Ideas in which sensibles participate, with mathematical objects as intermediates and the One and the great-and-small as principles. Aristotle concludes that no predecessor named a cause beyond his four, that essence was only hinted at by the Platonists, and that the final cause was treated only incidentally.","ideas":["Pythagoreans made number the matter and substance of things","Plato's Ideas arose from Socratic definition and Heraclitean flux","Plato used only the formal and material causes","No predecessor found a fifth kind of cause"],"terms":[{"t":"methexis (participation)","d":"Plato's term for the relation of sensibles to Ideas, which Aristotle calls a renaming of Pythagorean imitation."},{"t":"the great and the small","d":"The dyad Plato treated as material principle, from which Numbers arise by participation in the One."}]},{"i":50,"j":60,"loc":"Book I, part 4","title":"Criticism of physicists, Pythagoreans and Forms","summary":"Aristotle turns to the difficulties in each view. Material monists ignore incorporeal things, the moving cause and essence, and do not settle how the elements arise from one another; Empedocles abolishes qualitative change; Anaxagoras, pieced together, would mean the One and an indefinite Other. The Pythagoreans give no account of movement, weight or how abstract numbers relate to the numbers making up the heavens. Against the Platonists he argues that positing Forms doubles what is to be explained, that the arguments for them prove either nothing or Forms of negations, perishables and relations, and that the more accurate arguments lead to the third man.","ideas":["Monists omit incorporeal being, motion and essence","Pythagorean numbers cannot explain movement or weight","Forms multiply rather than explain things","Arguments for Forms prove too much or lead to the third man"],"terms":[{"t":"one over many","d":"The Platonist argument that a single Form exists for each group of things sharing a name."},{"t":"third man","d":"The objection that a Form and its instances require a further Form, without end."}]},{"i":61,"j":80,"loc":"Book I, part 5","title":"Forms explain nothing; the review concludes","summary":"Aristotle presses that Forms contribute nothing to sensibles: they cause no movement, are not in things, and calling them patterns is empty words and poetic metaphor. He objects that a substance cannot exist apart from what it is the substance of, that houses and rings come to be without Forms, and that Ideal Numbers, their units, intermediates and the derivation of lines and planes from great-and-small all fail. The Platonists have abandoned the moving and final causes and turned philosophy into mathematics. A search for elements of all things is impossible without distinguishing the senses of being, and no one could learn such a science; the earliest philosophy merely lisps the four causes.","ideas":["Forms neither move nor explain knowledge or being of sensibles","Patterns and participation are empty metaphors","Number-Forms and their units generate absurdities","Elements of all things cannot be sought without the senses of being"],"terms":[{"t":"paradeigma (pattern)","d":"The Platonist description of Forms as models copied by sensibles, rejected as metaphor."},{"t":"intermediates","d":"Mathematical objects placed between Forms and sensibles, eternal but many of a kind."}]},{"i":81,"j":93,"loc":"Book II","title":"Truth, first causes and no infinite regress","summary":"Truth is hard to reach whole but easy in part, since everyone contributes something, and the difficulty lies in us, as bats' eyes in daylight. Philosophy is rightly called knowledge of truth, and the principles of eternal things are most true. Aristotle argues that causes cannot form an infinite series, either in matter, moving causes, final causes or essence, for without a first there is no cause, without an end there is no Good, and infinite definitions make knowledge impossible. He closes on method: hearers expect what they are used to, mathematical precision suits only immaterial things, and one must first learn how to take each kind of argument.","ideas":["Inquiry into truth is a shared, cumulative work","As a thing is in being, so it is in truth","No infinite series in any of the four kinds of cause","Mathematical precision is not to be demanded of nature"],"terms":[{"t":"ad infinitum regress","d":"An endless series of causes, which Aristotle argues would abolish causation and knowledge."}]},{"i":94,"j":106,"loc":"Book III, part 1","title":"Listing the problems; one science or many","summary":"Aristotle explains that one must survey the difficulties first, since a knot cannot be untied unseen, and lists fourteen problems: whether one science studies all causes, axioms, all substances and attributes; whether only sensible substances exist; whether genera or constituents are principles; whether anything exists apart from concrete things; whether being and unity are substances; and whether principles are universal, potential or actual. He then argues both sides of the first problems. Unchangeable things have no final or moving cause, so mathematics ignores the good, yet each cause seems to claim Wisdom. It is also unclear whether the axioms, such as non-contradiction, belong to the science of substance, and whether one science can study all substances and their attributes.","ideas":["Stating difficulties well is the precondition of solving them","Fourteen problems set the agenda of the inquiry","Each of the four causes has a claim to be the object of Wisdom","It is disputed whether axioms and attributes fall under the science of substance"],"terms":[{"t":"aporia (difficulty)","d":"A puzzle where thought is bound and cannot go forward, to be stated before inquiry."},{"t":"axioms","d":"Common beliefs on which all proofs rest, such as that a thing cannot both be and not be."}]},{"i":107,"j":121,"loc":"Book III, part 2","title":"Intermediates, genera and things apart","summary":"Aristotle attacks the Platonist view that Forms are eternal sensibles and that mathematical intermediates exist, since astronomy would then need a second heaven, sun and moon; yet neither are perceptible lines those of the geometer, and placing intermediates inside sensibles puts two solids in one place. He next asks whether principles are genera or a thing's constituents, and whether the highest or the lowest genera, noting that being and unity cannot be genera. The hardest problem follows: if nothing exists apart from individuals there can be no knowledge or eternal thing, but if something does, it is unclear for which things and how the substance of many individuals can be one. He ends on whether principles are one in kind or one in number.","ideas":["Mathematical intermediates lead to a second heaven","Genera and constituent parts compete as principles","Being and unity cannot be genera","Knowledge needs something beyond individuals, yet this raises paradoxes"],"terms":[{"t":"genos (genus)","d":"A class predicated of species, here weighed against parts as the true principle."},{"t":"numerically one","d":"Being one individual, as opposed to one only in kind."}]},{"i":122,"j":150,"loc":"Book III, part 3","title":"Perishable principles, being, unity, numbers","summary":"Aristotle asks whether perishable and imperishable things have the same principles, dismisses Hesiod's nectar and ambrosia, and argues from Empedocles' verses that strife creates as much as it destroys. Either answer brings difficulties. He then takes up the question whether being and unity are the substance of things, as Plato and the Pythagoreans held, or have another nature: if not, no universal is substance and number vanishes; if so, Parmenides' result that all is one follows, and Zeno's argument threatens the indivisible unit. He weighs whether numbers, planes, lines and points are substances, why Forms are posited beyond intermediates, and whether principles are potential or actual and universal or individual.","ideas":["It is unexplained why the same elements yield perishable and eternal things","Making being and unity substances risks Parmenidean monism","Limits like points and surfaces cannot come to be or perish","Universal principles would not be substances; individual ones would not be knowable"],"terms":[{"t":"ousia (substance)","d":"A 'this', not predicated of a subject; what the problems ask the principles to be."},{"t":"dunamis (potency)","d":"Capability of being, whose priority would mean the principles might not be."}]},{"i":151,"j":160,"loc":"Book IV, part 1","title":"The science of being as being","summary":"Aristotle declares that there is a science of being as being and its essential attributes, distinct from the special sciences that cut off a part of being. Being is said in many senses, but all refer to one central point, substance, as healthy things all refer to health; so one science can study them all, and chiefly substance. Since being and unity go together, the same science studies the same, other, like, contrary and the other attributes, while sophists and dialecticians only seem to share its field. The axioms used by all sciences also belong to the philosopher, since they hold of being as such and physics is not the first Wisdom.","ideas":["Being is said in many senses relative to one, substance","One science studies being as being, unity and contraries","Philosophy differs from dialectic in power and from sophistic in aim","The philosopher, not the special scientist, examines the axioms"],"terms":[{"t":"on hēi on (being qua being)","d":"Being considered just as being, the subject of first philosophy."},{"t":"pros hen (relation to one)","d":"The way many senses of a word refer to one primary meaning, as healthy to health."}]},{"i":161,"j":169,"loc":"Book IV, part 2","title":"The principle of non-contradiction defended","summary":"Aristotle names the most certain principle: the same attribute cannot at once belong and not belong to the same subject in the same respect, and no one can truly believe the contrary. It cannot be demonstrated, since not everything can be, but it can be shown negatively if the opponent will only say something meaningful; one who says nothing is no better than a vegetable. If a word such as man signifies one thing, being a man cannot mean not being a man. Those who deny the principle abolish substance and essence and make every predicate accidental, which would require an impossible infinite chain of accidents.","ideas":["Non-contradiction is the firmest, non-hypothetical principle","It is shown by refutation once the opponent signifies something","Meaning one thing excludes meaning its opposite","Denying it destroys essence and makes everything accidental"],"terms":[{"t":"elenchos (negative demonstration)","d":"A refutation drawn from the opponent's own assertion rather than a proof from premisses."},{"t":"to ti ēn einai (essence)","d":"What it is to be a thing, which the deniers of the principle must abolish."}]},{"i":170,"j":181,"loc":"Book IV, part 3","title":"Absurd results; Protagoras and the senses","summary":"If contradictories are all true, all things become one, a trireme, a wall and a man, which yields Anaxagoras's mixture and amounts to speaking of the indeterminate. The opponent ends up saying neither yes nor no, and his own conduct refutes him: he walks to Megara and avoids wells and precipices. Even if all is so and not so, there is a more and less of truth. Aristotle then ties the doctrine to Protagoras and distinguishes those troubled by real difficulties from those arguing for argument's sake. The sincere ones were led by seeing contraries arise from the same thing and by conflicting sense impressions, as Democritus and Empedocles supposed knowledge to be sensation.","ideas":["Denying non-contradiction makes all things one","Action reveals that no one truly holds the view","Degrees of error imply a norm of truth","Belief in contradictions grew from observing the sensible world"],"terms":[{"t":"to aoriston (the indeterminate)","d":"What exists only potentially, which the deniers mistake for being."}]},{"i":182,"j":197,"loc":"Book IV, part 4","title":"Flux, appearances and the measure of truth","summary":"Aristotle cites Empedocles, Parmenides, Anaxagoras and Homer as treating thought as changing with bodily state, and notes how this would dishearten beginners. Their error was identifying being with the sensible world, full of the indeterminate and change; Cratylus finally only moved his finger. Aristotle replies that change presupposes something that is, that we know things by form rather than quantity, that the changing region is a fraction of the universe, and that something changeless exists. Against the claim that every appearance is true, he argues that appearance differs from sensation, that each sense is authoritative over its proper object, that the expert's forecast outweighs the layman's, and that the cause of sensation must exist beforehand; those who still insist must make everything relative.","ideas":["Equating being with the sensible world led to flux doctrines","Change presupposes a persisting subject and form","Not every appearance is true; senses have proper objects","The demand for proof of everything is itself a mistake"],"terms":[{"t":"phantasia (appearance)","d":"How things seem, which Aristotle distinguishes from sensation itself."}]},{"i":198,"j":202,"loc":"Book IV, part 5","title":"Excluded middle and self-refuting theses","summary":"From non-contradiction Aristotle infers that contraries cannot belong to the same thing at once. He then argues that there is no intermediate between contradictories, defining truth as saying of what is that it is and of what is not that it is not, and showing that an intermediate would lead to absurd changes and an endless series. Heraclitus's doctrine seems to make everything true and Anaxagoras's everything false; both theses destroy themselves. The book ends by rejecting the claims that all things are at rest or all in motion, since a first mover is itself unmoved.","ideas":["Contraries cannot belong to one subject at once","Between contradictories there is no middle","'All is true' and 'all is false' refute themselves","Not everything rests or moves; the first mover is unmoved"],"terms":[{"t":"excluded middle","d":"The principle that of one subject one must either affirm or deny any predicate."},{"t":"alētheia (truth)","d":"Saying of what is that it is, and of what is not that it is not."}]},{"i":203,"j":218,"loc":"Book V, part 1","title":"Beginning, cause, element, nature, necessary","summary":"Book V is a lexicon of philosophical terms. 'Beginning' has six senses, all sharing the idea of a first point from which a thing is, comes to be or is known. 'Cause' is distinguished into matter, form, source of change and end, with remarks on things causing each other, accidental and potential causes, and Polyclitus as an accidental cause of the statue. 'Element' is the primary indivisible component; 'nature' ranges from growth and matter to essence, and strictly means the essence of things that have a source of movement in themselves. 'Necessary' covers conditions, compulsion and what cannot be otherwise, the simple being necessary in the primary sense.","ideas":["All beginnings are first points of being, becoming or knowing","The four causes and their many varieties","Nature strictly is the essence of self-moving things","The necessary primarily means what cannot be otherwise"],"terms":[{"t":"aitia (cause)","d":"Matter, form, source of change, or end, each answering a different 'why'."},{"t":"phusis (nature)","d":"Most strictly, the essence of things having in themselves a source of movement."},{"t":"stoicheion (element)","d":"The primary immanent component, not divisible into things of another kind."}]},{"i":219,"j":234,"loc":"Book V, part 2","title":"One, being and substance","summary":"Aristotle distinguishes what is one by accident, such as musical Coriscus, from what is one by nature: by continuity, by sameness of substratum or genus, or by indivisible definition, with the whole and the circle as most truly one. The essence of one is to be a beginning and measure of number, and things are one in number, species, genus or by analogy. 'Being' is said accidentally, according to the figures of predication, as truth, and as potential or actual. 'Substance' means the simple bodies and what is composed of them, the cause of being, limiting parts, and essence, reducible to the ultimate substratum and the separable form.","ideas":["Unity by continuity, kind, genus and definition","The one is the first measure of each class","Being follows the categories, truth, and potency and actuality","Substance is the substratum and the separable form"],"terms":[{"t":"katēgoriai (figures of predication)","d":"What, quality, quantity, relation, acting, being acted on, where, when, each a sense of being."},{"t":"kata sumbebēkos (accidentally)","d":"Said through something that merely happens to belong to a subject."}]},{"i":235,"j":250,"loc":"Book V, part 3","title":"Same, contrary, prior and potency","summary":"Aristotle defines the same, other, different, like and unlike, then opposites and contraries, noting that same, other and contrary vary with each category as one and being do. 'Prior' and 'posterior' apply by nearness to a beginning in place, time, movement, power and arrangement, by priority for knowledge, and by nature and substance, a distinction he credits to Plato. 'Potency' is a source of change in another thing or in the same thing qua other, with related senses of passive, good and resistant capacity. Incapacity is its privation, and 'possible' and 'impossible' also have senses with no reference to potency, as with the incommensurable diagonal.","ideas":["Sameness is a unity of being of more than one thing","Contrariety is the greatest difference within a genus","Priority by nature: what can be without the others","Primary potency is a source of change in another or in itself qua other"],"terms":[{"t":"enantia (contraries)","d":"The most different things within the same genus or receptive subject."},{"t":"dunamis (potency)","d":"A source of movement or change in another thing or in the same thing qua other."}]},{"i":251,"j":264,"loc":"Book V, part 4","title":"Quantum, quality, relative, complete, limit","summary":"A quantum is divisible into parts, as plurality if numerable and magnitude if measurable, some things being quanta by nature and others incidentally, as movement and time. Quality is primarily the differentia of essence, and secondarily the modifications of moving things, including virtue and vice. Relatives are numerical, active and passive, or measurable and knowable, the last relative only because something else refers to them. Aristotle then defines the complete, the limit, 'that in virtue of which', and 'in virtue of itself', tying each to form, end and essence.","ideas":["Plurality and magnitude are the two kinds of quantum","Quality chiefly means the differentia of essence","Three kinds of relative, one of which is relative only derivatively","Completeness and limit are linked to the end and the essence"],"terms":[{"t":"poson (quantum)","d":"What is divisible into constituents each of which is a one and a this."},{"t":"pros ti (relative)","d":"What is what it is by reference to something else."},{"t":"teleion (complete)","d":"Lacking no part, unexcelled in its kind, or having attained its end."}]},{"i":265,"j":274,"loc":"Book V, part 5","title":"Disposition, having, privation, part and whole","summary":"Aristotle defines disposition as arrangement of parts, 'having' as an activity between haver and had and as a habit such as health, and affection as alterable quality and painful alteration. Privation has several senses, from the plant lacking eyes to blindness at the age one should see, and corresponds to words with negative prefixes; hence intermediate states exist between good and bad. He sets out the senses of 'have' or 'hold', 'being in', and 'coming from'. He defines 'part' as what a quantum or kind divides into, and 'whole' as what lacks none of its parts and makes them a unity, by nature more than by art.","ideas":["Habit is a disposition well or ill ordered","Privation is lacking what one would naturally have","'Coming from' covers matter, moving cause, whole and succession","A whole contains its parts as a unity"],"terms":[{"t":"hexis (having, habit)","d":"A disposition by which a thing is well or ill disposed, as health."},{"t":"sterēsis (privation)","d":"The absence of an attribute where and when it would naturally be present."}]},{"i":275,"j":283,"loc":"Book V, part 6","title":"Totals, genus, falsity and accident","summary":"Aristotle distinguishes totals, where position makes no difference, from wholes, and explains that only a continuous whole with positioned, non-regenerating extremities can be mutilated, so baldness is not mutilation. 'Genus' or race means continuous generation, a first begetter, the substratum of differentiae, and the first element of a definition. 'False' applies to non-existent states of affairs, misleading things like dreams, accounts applied to the wrong object, and the man fond of such accounts; he faults Antisthenes and the argument of the Hippias. The book ends with 'accident': what holds neither necessarily nor usually, like finding treasure while digging, and what belongs per se but is outside the essence.","ideas":["Wholes differ from totals by the relevance of position","Genus as lineage, substratum and part of definition","Falsity in things, in accounts and in persons","Accident as chance attribute or non-essential per se attribute"],"terms":[{"t":"sumbebēkos (accident)","d":"What belongs to a thing neither necessarily nor usually, or per se but outside its essence."},{"t":"pseudos (the false)","d":"What does not exist, appears as what it is not, or is said of the wrong thing."}]},{"i":284,"j":291,"loc":"Book VI","title":"First philosophy, theology, and accidental being","summary":"Aristotle distinguishes the science of being qua being from the particular sciences, which take their genus and its essence for granted. Thought is practical, productive or theoretical, and there are three theoretical philosophies: physics (separate but movable things), mathematics (immovable but probably not separate things) and theology (things both separate and immovable). If there is an immovable substance, its science is first philosophy and universal because it is first. He then sets aside two senses of being: the accidental, of which there is no science because science concerns what is always or for the most part, and being as truth, which lies in the combining and separating done by thought, not in things.","ideas":["Physics, mathematics and theology are the three theoretical sciences","Natural things are defined like 'snub', always with matter","The accidental is what happens neither always nor for the most part, and no science treats it","Truth and falsity are in thought, not in things"],"terms":[{"t":"theologikē (theology)","d":"The first theoretical science, concerned with what is eternal, immovable and separate."},{"t":"to kata symbebēkos (the accidental)","d":"What belongs to a thing neither of necessity nor usually, with an indeterminate cause."}]},{"i":292,"j":302,"loc":"Book VII, part 1 of 6","title":"Substance as primary being; the substratum","summary":"Aristotle argues that 'being' is said primarily of the 'what' or substance, on which all other categories depend, and that substance is first in definition, knowledge and time. The old question 'what is being?' is therefore the question 'what is substance?'. He lists candidate substances (bodies, their limits, Plato's Forms and mathematicals, Speusippus' many kinds) and four candidates for the substance of a thing: essence, universal, genus and substratum. Taking the substratum first, he shows that stripping away all attributes leaves bare matter, but rejects matter as substance because substance must be separable and a 'this'. Form is then the most perplexing candidate, to be approached from sensible substances.","ideas":["All other categories are said to be through their relation to substance","Substance is prior in definition, knowledge and time","Matter, form and compound are each called substratum","Matter fails as substance because it lacks separability and 'thisness'"],"terms":[{"t":"ousia (substance)","d":"That which is primarily and without qualification, on which the other categories depend."},{"t":"hypokeimenon (substratum)","d":"That of which everything else is predicated while it is predicated of nothing else."}]},{"i":303,"j":318,"loc":"Book VII, part 2 of 6","title":"Essence, definition, and identity with essence","summary":"Aristotle examines essence, what a thing is said to be in virtue of itself. Strictly only species of a genus, that is substances, have essences and definitions; compounds like 'white man' (called 'cloak') and coupled terms like 'snub' have them only in a secondary way or lead to absurd repetitions. Like 'is', 'definition' and 'essence' are used primarily of substance and derivatively of the other categories, as 'medical' is used with reference to one thing. He then argues that each primary, self-subsistent thing is the same as its essence, since otherwise knowledge of it would be impossible and an infinite regress of essences would follow; accidental unities are not the same as their essences.","ideas":["Only substance is definable in the primary sense","'Snub nose' shows the difficulty of defining coupled terms","If Ideas differed from their essences, knowledge and being would be severed","Primary things are one with their essence; accidental compounds are not"],"terms":[{"t":"to ti ēn einai (essence)","d":"What a thing is in virtue of itself, expressed in its definition."},{"t":"horismos (definition)","d":"The formula of the essence, belonging chiefly to substances."}]},{"i":319,"j":331,"loc":"Book VII, part 3 of 6","title":"Coming to be by nature, art, and chance","summary":"Everything that comes to be does so by something, from something, and becomes something; Aristotle analyses natural generation, production by art and spontaneous generation. In art the form is in the artist's soul: the doctor reasons back from health to what he can do, and the making then proceeds forward, so in a sense health comes from health and house from house. Neither the matter nor the form is produced; what is made is the concrete compound, bringing form into this matter. Separate Forms are therefore useless as causes of generation, for the begetter, of the same kind as the offspring, suffices ('man begets man'). Some things arise spontaneously because their matter can move itself in the needed way.","ideas":["Thinking runs from the form back to a first step; making runs forward from it","Form is not generated; only the compound of form and matter is","Platonic Forms are unnecessary as patterns for generation","Substance requires a pre-existing actual substance to produce it"],"terms":[{"t":"eidos (form)","d":"The essence and primary substance of a thing, which is not itself produced."},{"t":"to automaton (the spontaneous)","d":"Coming to be without art, possible when the matter can initiate the required motion itself."}]},{"i":332,"j":348,"loc":"Book VII, part 4 of 6","title":"Parts of the formula and parts of the thing","summary":"Aristotle asks whether the formulae of the parts belong in the formula of the whole, and which parts are prior. Material parts (bronze of a statue, flesh of Callias, segments of a circle) are parts of the concrete thing, not of the form, and are posterior; parts of the formula are prior. The soul is the substance of an animal according to its formula, and a severed finger is a finger only in name. It is hard to tell which parts are material when a form is always found in the same matter, but reducing everything to form, as some do with numbers, is useless, since an animal cannot be defined without movement and organs in working order. Individuals have no definition and are known by intuition or perception; there is perceptible and intelligible matter.","ideas":["Material parts belong to the compound, formal parts to the definition","Soul is the primary substance; body is matter","Defining man without flesh and bones is not like defining circle without bronze","Intelligible matter is present in mathematical objects"],"terms":[{"t":"hylē noētē (intelligible matter)","d":"The matter present in mathematical objects, not perceptible as such."},{"t":"synolon (concrete whole)","d":"The compound of form and matter, which alone resolves into material parts."}]},{"i":349,"j":363,"loc":"Book VII, part 5 of 6","title":"Unity of definition; universals are not substance","summary":"Aristotle first asks why a definition like 'two-footed animal' is one thing, and answers for definitions by division: if each step divides by a differentia of the previous differentia, the last differentia is the substance and the definition. He then turns to the universal, which some take to be a cause and principle. A universal cannot be substance, since substance is peculiar to its thing and not predicated of a subject, whereas the universal is common and predicated; a common term indicates a 'such', not a 'this', and the 'third man' difficulty follows otherwise. Nor can a substance consist of actual substances. He draws out absurdities for those who make separate Ideas consist of genus and differentiae.","ideas":["Correct division makes the last differentia the whole definition","No universal is substance; it signifies a 'such', not a 'this'","A substance cannot be composed of actually present substances","Separate Forms of animal lead to contradictions whether one or many"],"terms":[{"t":"diaphora (differentia)","d":"The difference that divides a genus into species, the last of which gives the definition."},{"t":"katholou (universal)","d":"What is by nature predicated of many things."}]},{"i":364,"j":373,"loc":"Book VII, part 6 of 6","title":"Indefinability of individuals; substance as cause","summary":"Sensible individuals, having perishable matter, admit neither definition nor demonstration, and Ideas, said to be individuals, cannot be defined either, since words are common and their elements would be predicable of many; the sun is an example. Most supposed substances, like animal parts and the elements, are only potencies, and neither unity nor being is substance. The Platonists rightly make substances separate but wrongly make the 'one over many' a Form. Making a new start, Aristotle treats substance as a cause: inquiry asks why matter is some definite thing, such as why these bricks are a house. The answer is the form, which, like the syllable beyond its letters, is not an element but a principle.","ideas":["There is no definition of perishable individuals or of Ideas","Being and unity, being common, are not substances","To ask 'why' is to ask why something belongs to something","Form is the cause of unity, not a further element"],"terms":[{"t":"aitia (cause)","d":"That by reason of which matter is some definite thing, identified here with substance as form."},{"t":"stoicheion (element)","d":"That into which a thing is divided and which is present in it as matter."}]},{"i":374,"j":386,"loc":"Book VIII, part 1 of 2","title":"Sensible substance as matter and actuality","summary":"Aristotle summarises the results so far and returns to the generally recognized sensible substances, all of which have matter. Matter is substance as what underlies change and is potentially a 'this'. Against Democritus' three differences, he lists many kinds of differentiae (blending, binding, position, time, place, sensible affections), which are analogous to substance and serve as the actuality of the matter, as in defining a threshold or ice. Definitions by components give the matter, by function the actuality, and combined ones the compound. The form is not a further element; the Antisthenean puzzle shows that only composites are definable, and a definition is like a number in being indivisibly one.","ideas":["Matter underlies every kind of change, including generation","Differentiae such as position or mixture act as actuality in definitions","A house as bricks is potential, as shelter is actual","Definition, like number, changes if anything is added or removed"],"terms":[{"t":"energeia (actuality)","d":"What is predicated of matter as its realized form or arrangement."},{"t":"dynamis (potency)","d":"The status of matter as what can become a definite 'this'."}]},{"i":387,"j":394,"loc":"Book VIII, part 2 of 2","title":"Proximate matter and the unity of definition","summary":"Aristotle notes that each thing has a proximate matter of its own, and that one should state all four causes, naming the nearest, as in man, eclipse and sleep. Things that are without coming to be, such as points and forms, have no matter, and contrary states relate to matter differently, as wine to vinegar or the living to the corpse. He returns to the puzzle of why a definition such as 'animal + biped' is one. The difficulty dissolves once one element is seen as matter and the other as form, one potential and the other actual. Proximate matter and form are the same thing, and nothing else causes unity except what moves potency into actuality.","ideas":["Seek the proximate causes, not remote matter like fire or earth","Eclipse is defined with its cause, the earth's interposition","Things without matter are essentially unities","Theories of 'participation' or 'communion' miss that potency and act are one"],"terms":[{"t":"eschatē hylē (proximate matter)","d":"The matter nearest to the form, one with it as potency is with act."}]},{"i":395,"j":404,"loc":"Book IX, part 1 of 3","title":"Potency, rational powers, and the Megarians","summary":"Aristotle turns to potency and actuality, beginning with potency in the strict sense, an originative source of change in another or in the thing itself as other. Active and passive potencies are in a sense one and in a sense distinct, and incapacity is the corresponding privation. Rational potencies, such as the arts, can produce contrary effects, as medicine can produce health or disease, while non-rational ones produce one. He then attacks the Megaric view that a thing can act only when it is acting: it would follow that a builder not building lacks his art, that nothing is perceptible unless perceived, as in Protagoras' doctrine, and that people are blind many times a day.","ideas":["Primary potency is a source of change in another or in the self as other","Rational powers are capable of contraries; non-rational of one effect","The Megaric view collapses potency into actuality","It would make arts unlearnable and lost each time they are unused"],"terms":[{"t":"dynamis (potency, capacity)","d":"An originative source of change in another thing or in the thing itself qua other."},{"t":"Megarikoi (Megarians)","d":"The school holding that a thing can act only when it actually acts."}]},{"i":405,"j":415,"loc":"Book IX, part 2 of 3","title":"Possibility, actuality, and when things are potential","summary":"Aristotle concludes against the Megarians that potency and actuality differ, which preserves motion and becoming, and defines the capable as that whose actualization involves nothing impossible. He distinguishes the false from the impossible and shows that if A's reality entails B's, A's possibility entails B's. Rational potencies act when desire or choice decides. Actuality is illustrated by analogy (building to able to build, waking to sleeping, seeing to having sight), and movements, which are incomplete, are contrasted with actualities like seeing and thinking, where one is doing and has done at once. Earth is not yet potentially a man, but seed in the right state is; the 'thaten' usage leads back to prime matter.","ideas":["The false is not the same as the impossible","Desire or choice decides which contrary a rational power produces","Movement is incomplete; actuality has its end present","Something is potential only when nothing must be added or removed"],"terms":[{"t":"kinēsis (movement)","d":"An incomplete process, such as walking or building, that is not yet its end."},{"t":"prōtē hylē (prime matter)","d":"The first subject that is no longer called 'thaten' of anything further."}]},{"i":416,"j":433,"loc":"Book IX, part 3 of 3","title":"Priority of actuality; truth about incomposites","summary":"Aristotle argues that actuality is prior to potency in formula, in time (since an actual thing of the same kind always produces the potential one, back to the eternal prime mover) and in substance, because the end and form are actuality and potency exists for its sake. Eternal things, being imperishable, have no potency for not being, so the heavens never tire. Actuality is also better than good potency, which is equally capable of contraries, so there is nothing bad among eternal things; geometrical truths are found by actualizing constructions. Finally, truth as being concerns combined and separated things, while about incomposite essences there is no error, only contact with them or ignorance.","ideas":["Actuality is prior in formula, time and substance","Eternal things exist actually and have no potency to not be","The good actuality is better than the potency for contraries","Of simple essences one either grasps them or is ignorant, never in error"],"terms":[{"t":"entelecheia (complete reality)","d":"The completed state toward which potency is directed, linked to 'action'."},{"t":"thigein (contact)","d":"The direct grasp of incomposites, which is truth in their case."}]},{"i":434,"j":441,"loc":"Book X, part 1 of 4","title":"The senses of one; unity as measure","summary":"Aristotle lists the main senses of 'one': the continuous, the whole, the individual and the universal, all one because their movement or thought is indivisible. He distinguishes what things are called one from what it is to be one, which is chiefly to be the first measure of a kind, above all of quantity. Units of measure in length, weight, motion, music (the quarter-tone) and speech (the letter) are indivisible, exactly or to perception, and the measure is homogeneous with what it measures. Knowledge and perception are called measures but are rather measured by things, so Protagoras' saying that man is the measure only appears profound.","ideas":["Four main kinds of unity: continuous, whole, individual, universal","To be one is chiefly to be the first measure of a kind","The measure is always of the same kind as the measured","Protagoras' 'man the measure' says nothing remarkable"],"terms":[{"t":"hen (one, unity)","d":"The indivisible, especially as the first measure of a class."},{"t":"metron (measure)","d":"That by which the quantity or quality of a thing is first known."}]},{"i":442,"j":457,"loc":"Book X, part 2 of 4","title":"The one is not substance; contrariety","summary":"Aristotle asks whether the one is a substance, as the Pythagoreans and Plato held, or an attribute of some underlying nature. Since no universal is substance and being and unity are the most universal predicates, the one cannot be a substance or a genus; in each category the one is some definite thing, as white among colours or the quarter-tone among tunes. One and many are opposed as indivisible and divisible, and the same, the like and the equal belong to the one, the other, the unlike and the unequal to plurality. He distinguishes otherness from difference and argues that contrariety is the greatest, complete difference, that each thing has one contrary, and that the primary contrariety is between a positive state and privation.","ideas":["Unity, like being, is not a substance or genus","In every category the one is a particular thing, not bare unity","Difference requires a shared genus or species; otherness does not","Contrariety is complete difference, rooted in state and privation"],"terms":[{"t":"enantiōsis (contrariety)","d":"The greatest or complete difference between things in the same genus."},{"t":"sterēsis (privation)","d":"The absence of a positive state in what is receptive of it, the root of contrariety."}]},{"i":458,"j":466,"loc":"Book X, part 3 of 4","title":"The equal, one and many, and intermediates","summary":"Aristotle asks how the equal is opposed to the great and the small, since one thing has one contrary, and concludes that it is their privative negation, neither great nor small but fit to be either. He then works through puzzles about how the one is opposed to the many: 'many' means either excessive plurality or number, and in the latter sense alone it is opposed to the one, as measured to measure; two is the first plurality, and he corrects Anaxagoras on fewness. Finally he argues that intermediates are in the same genus as their extremes, stand between contraries, and are compounded of the contraries, as colours between white and black.","ideas":["The equal is a privative negation of both great and small","One is opposed to many as measure to the measurable","Two is the first plurality and, without qualification, few","Intermediates are composed of the contraries they stand between"],"terms":[{"t":"metaxy (intermediate)","d":"What a changing thing passes through first, lying between contraries in the same genus."}]},{"i":467,"j":474,"loc":"Book X, part 4 of 4","title":"Difference in species; perishable and imperishable","summary":"Things other in species share a genus whose very nature differs in each, and this difference is a contrariety within the genus. Aristotle asks why male and female, though contraries, do not make different species, and answers that contrarieties in the definition make specific differences while those in the matter, like paleness or sex, do not. He then argues that perishable and imperishable, being contraries that belong of necessity rather than by accident, must differ in kind. Hence there cannot be Forms of the kind some maintain, for the Form and the individual would then be the same in form yet one perishable and the other imperishable.","ideas":["Difference in species is a contrariety within one genus","Contrarieties in the matter do not make different species","Perishability belongs to a thing necessarily, not by accident","Imperishable Forms cannot share a form with perishable individuals"],"terms":[{"t":"genos (genus)","d":"The identical thing predicated of species that differ, functioning as their matter."},{"t":"phtharton (perishable)","d":"A necessary attribute in the essence of perishable things, opposed in kind to the imperishable."}]},{"i":475,"j":492,"loc":"Book XI (1 of 5)","title":"Puzzles about Wisdom and the science of being","summary":"Aristotle restates the difficulties about Wisdom: whether it is one science or many, whether it studies the principles of demonstration, all substances, attributes as well as substances, the Forms, mathematicals, or perceptible things. He asks whether its principles are elements or highest genera such as being and unity, whether anything exists apart from individuals, and how eternal and perishable things could share a principle. He then answers the first puzzle: 'being' has many senses, but all are said with reference to one thing, as 'medical' and 'healthy' are, so one science can study being qua being and its contrarieties. As the mathematician abstracts quantity, the philosopher considers things only as being, and physics and mathematics count as parts of Wisdom.","ideas":["A string of aporiai about the scope and objects of Wisdom","Being and unity cannot be genera, since a genus is not predicated of its differentiae","Being is said in many senses but in reference to one, like 'healthy'","First philosophy also examines the common axioms used by mathematics"],"terms":[{"t":"being qua being","d":"Things considered only in so far as they are, the proper object of the philosopher."},{"t":"pros hen (in reference to one)","d":"Use of a word in many senses that all refer to one common thing, as with 'healthy'."}]},{"i":493,"j":507,"loc":"Book XI (2 of 5)","title":"Contradiction, Heraclitus, and Protagoras' measure doctrine","summary":"Aristotle states the principle that the same thing cannot at once be and not be, which has no full proof but can be defended against an opponent who says anything meaningful. If words signify one thing, contradictories cannot both be true; otherwise a man would also be a horse. He applies this to Heraclitus and to Protagoras' claim that man is the measure, tracing such views to physical theories of coming-to-be and to differing appearances. Healthy perceivers, the unchanging heavens, the reality of motion, and everyday conduct such as eating bread all count against them. He concludes that neither contradictories nor contraries, nor an intermediate, can be truly said of one subject, and that not all statements can be true or all false.","ideas":["No demonstration of the principle of contradiction, only refutation of the denier","Meaningful speech requires words to signify one thing","The perception of the sound, not the impaired, is the measure","Anaxagoras' 'everything in everything' also violates the principle"],"terms":[{"t":"principle of non-contradiction","d":"The same thing cannot at the same time be and not be, or bear a similar pair of opposites."},{"t":"proof ad hominem","d":"Refutation drawn from the opponent's own admissions rather than from a prior principle."}]},{"i":508,"j":517,"loc":"Book XI (3 of 5)","title":"Kinds of science, theology, and the accidental","summary":"Every science takes the 'what' of its class by perception or hypothesis and does not demonstrate it. Natural science is theoretical, not practical or productive, and defines things like 'snub', with their matter. If there is a separable, unmovable substance, a third theoretical science deals with it; Aristotle names physics, mathematics, and theology, and ranks theology highest. No science studies being by accident, which only sophistic pursues; the accidental happens neither always nor for the most part, and its causes are indefinite. Luck is an accidental cause among events done for an end, and since nothing accidental is prior to the essential, reason and nature are causes before luck or spontaneity.","ideas":["Three theoretical sciences: physics, mathematics, theology","Natural definitions include matter, like 'snub' rather than 'concave'","There is no science of the accidental","Luck is an accidental cause, posterior to reason and nature"],"terms":[{"t":"theologikē (theology)","d":"The science of separable, unmovable substance, where the divine would be found."},{"t":"to sumbebēkos (the accidental)","d":"What happens neither always nor for the most part."}]},{"i":518,"j":525,"loc":"Book XI (4 of 5)","title":"Definition of movement and the infinite","summary":"Aristotle defines movement as the actuality of the potential as such, illustrated by the buildable being built: the act of building, not the house, is the movement. This explains why others called movement indefinite or classed it with otherness and inequality, since it is an incomplete actuality. Movement is in the movable, and the actuality of mover and moved is one, like the road up and down. He then argues that the infinite cannot exist as a separate substance or in actuality, and that there is no infinite sensible body, whether composite or simple, because of the balance of contraries, proper places, and the six directions. Infinity in movement and time is derivative from infinity in magnitude.","ideas":["Movement is the actuality of the potential qua potential","The actuality of mover and moved is one but differs in being","The infinite is an attribute, not a separable principle","No infinite body can exist, since bodies have places and limits"],"terms":[{"t":"kinēsis (movement)","d":"The complete reality of what exists potentially, as potential."},{"t":"apeiron (the infinite)","d":"That which cannot be traversed; here shown not to exist actually or separately."}]},{"i":526,"j":530,"loc":"Book XI (5 of 5)","title":"Kinds of change and terms of continuity","summary":"Aristotle distinguishes accidental, partial, and essential change, and notes that the terminals of change, such as forms and places, are themselves unmovable. Change runs between positive and negative terms: generation and destruction are changes between contradictories and are not movements, so movement is only from positive to positive. There is movement only in quality, quantity, and place, not in substance, relation, or action, and there is no change of change, which would lead to an infinite regress. He defines rest as privation of movement in what can move, and ends by defining together, apart, touching, between, successive, contiguous, and continuous.","ideas":["Generation and destruction are changes but not movements","Movement occurs only in quality, quantity, and place","No change of change or becoming of becoming","Continuity is a kind of contiguity where limits become one"],"terms":[{"t":"metabolē (change)","d":"The wider class including generation, destruction, and movement proper."},{"t":"sunechēs (continuous)","d":"Things whose touching limits become one and the same."}]},{"i":531,"j":545,"loc":"Book XII (1 of 4)","title":"Sensible substance and its principles","summary":"Book XII opens by making substance the subject, since the other categories depend on it. There are three kinds of substance: perishable sensible, eternal sensible, and immovable; physics studies the first two. Change requires a persisting matter besides the contraries, and things come from what is potentially; the three principles are form, privation, and matter, while neither last matter nor last form comes to be. Substances arise from things of the same name by art, nature, luck, or spontaneity; man begets man, so Ideas are not needed as causes. The principles of all things are the same only by analogy: form, privation, matter, and moving cause, with actuality and potency, while actual causes are always individual.","ideas":["Three kinds of substance; the unmovable belongs to another science","Matter, form, and privation as principles of change","Man begets man: the moving cause is a like individual","Principles are the same for all things only analogically"],"terms":[{"t":"hulē (matter)","d":"What persists through change and is potentially both contraries."},{"t":"sterēsis (privation)","d":"The absence of a form, the contrary from which change starts."}]},{"i":546,"j":555,"loc":"Book XII (2 of 4)","title":"The unmoved mover and the life of God","summary":"Since movement and time are eternal and only circular motion is continuous, there must be an eternal substance whose essence is actuality, without matter. Aristotle rejects the priority of potency held by those who begin from night or 'all things together', and credits Leucippus, Plato, Anaxagoras, and Empedocles in part. The eternal circular motion of the first heaven requires a mover that moves without being moved, as objects of desire and thought do; it moves as being loved and exists of necessity. On this principle depend the heavens and nature; its life is thought thinking its object, eternal and most good, and this is God. Against the Pythagoreans and Speusippus, the complete comes before the seed, and the first mover is shown to be without magnitude and impassive.","ideas":["An eternal substance whose very essence is actuality","The first mover moves as object of love and thought","God's life is the activity of thought, eternal and best","The complete is prior to the seed"],"terms":[{"t":"akinēton kinoun (unmoved mover)","d":"The eternal substance that causes motion without itself being moved."},{"t":"energeia (actuality)","d":"Being-at-work; the essence of the first principle."}]},{"i":556,"j":563,"loc":"Book XII (3 of 4)","title":"How many unmoved movers: the celestial spheres","summary":"Aristotle asks whether there is one such substance or many, noting that the Platonists never justify their number of Ideas. Because each eternal planetary motion needs its own unmovable mover, the movers must equal the motions, and astronomy must count them. He reports Eudoxus' system of spheres and Callippus' additions, then adds counteracting spheres, reaching fifty-five, or forty-seven without some additions. All motion must serve the stars, so there are no further movers; the first mover is one, so there is one heaven. He closes with the ancestral tradition that the heavenly bodies are gods, separating this inherited core from later anthropomorphic myth.","ideas":["One mover for each eternal celestial motion","Eudoxus and Callippus on concentric spheres","Counteracting spheres give fifty-five in all","Only one heaven, since the first mover has no matter"],"terms":[{"t":"sphairai (spheres)","d":"Nested rotating bodies by which astronomers explain planetary motions."}]},{"i":564,"j":573,"loc":"Book XII (4 of 4)","title":"Thought thinking itself and the good of the universe","summary":"Aristotle raises problems about divine thought: if it thought nothing it would be like a sleeper, and if its substance were a potency, its object would be better than it. It must therefore think itself, as the most excellent thing, so that its thinking is a thinking on thinking, and in things without matter thought and object are one and indivisible. The universe holds the good both as a separate leader and as the order of its parts, like an army or a household. He then criticizes rival views that make contraries principles, Empedocles' love, Anaxagoras' reason, the Forms, and number theories that make the universe a series of episodes. The book ends with a verse against the rule of many.","ideas":["Divine thought thinks itself: thinking on thinking","The good exists as leader and as order, as in an army","Rivals cannot explain the good, becoming, or unity","The world refuses to be governed badly"],"terms":[{"t":"noēsis noēseōs (thinking of thinking)","d":"The self-directed activity of the divine thought."},{"t":"taxis (order)","d":"The arrangement of parts in which the universe's good partly consists."}]},{"i":574,"j":591,"loc":"Book XIII (1 of 5)","title":"How the objects of mathematics exist","summary":"Turning to whether there is unmovable substance besides the sensible, Aristotle distinguishes views that mathematicals are substances and that Ideas are, and examines mathematicals first. They cannot be in sensible things, since then bodies could not be divided, nor separate, since this multiplies solids, planes, lines, and points absurdly and would separate the objects of astronomy, optics, and harmonics too. Mathematicals are prior in definition but not in substance. His own answer is that mathematics studies sensible things not as sensible but as lengths, planes, or units, setting apart in thought what is not separate. Mathematics is not silent about the beautiful, since order, symmetry, and definiteness are its themes.","ideas":["Mathematicals are neither in sensibles nor separate from them","Prior in definition is not prior in substance","Mathematics studies sensibles qua quantities, by abstraction","Order, symmetry, and definiteness are chief forms of beauty"],"terms":[{"t":"aphairesis (abstraction)","d":"Treating separately in thought what is not separate in fact."},{"t":"prior in definition vs. in substance","d":"Two kinds of priority that do not coincide."}]},{"i":592,"j":600,"loc":"Book XIII (2 of 5)","title":"Origins and failures of the theory of Ideas","summary":"Aristotle examines the Ideas in their original form. The theory arose from Heraclitean flux combined with Socrates' search for universal definitions; Socrates did not separate universals, but his successors did and called them Ideas. The arguments for Forms either prove nothing or yield Forms of negations, perishables, and relations, and lead to the 'third man'. If Forms and particulars share a form, there is something common; if not, the name is merely equivocal. The Forms contribute nothing to motion, knowledge, or being; calling them patterns is empty metaphor, and the Phaedo's claim that they cause being and becoming fails without a moving cause.","ideas":["Socrates contributed induction and universal definition","Separating universals produced the Ideas","Arguments for Forms prove too much, including the 'third man'","Patterns and participation are poetical metaphors"],"terms":[{"t":"idea / eidos (Idea, Form)","d":"A universal given separate existence apart from particulars."},{"t":"third man","d":"An objection that Form and particular need a further common Form."}]},{"i":601,"j":612,"loc":"Book XIII (3 of 5)","title":"Ideal numbers and inassociable units","summary":"Aristotle surveys the ways number could be a separate substance: units all associable, all inassociable, or associable only within each number, and whether numbers are separable or in sensibles. He sorts existing views, including the Pythagoreans, who make sensible things out of numbers with magnitude. If all units are associable, only mathematical number exists and the Ideas cannot be numbers. If all are inassociable, number is neither mathematical nor ideal, and generation from the one and the indefinite dyad breaks down. Units differentiated only between numbers also lead to absurdities, as with the 10-itself and its 5's, and Ideas would be composed of Ideas.","ideas":["Three possible accounts of units in number","Undifferentiated units leave no room for ideal number","Inassociable units clash with counting by addition","Unity of a number cannot come from contact or mixture"],"terms":[{"t":"inassociable units","d":"Units that cannot be added to units of another number."},{"t":"indefinite dyad","d":"The Platonist material principle that, with the One, generates number."}]},{"i":613,"j":627,"loc":"Book XIII (4 of 5)","title":"Further absurdities in theories of number","summary":"Differentiating units is called a fiction, since units differ neither in quantity nor quality, and equal undifferentiated numbers are the same. Ideas cannot be numbers, and the view that ideal and mathematical number are the same is the worst; the Pythagorean version avoids some problems but builds bodies from units that have no magnitude. Aristotle asks whether units come from the great and small, whether number is infinite or limited to ten, and argues that the decade leaves too few Forms. He explains how 1 is prior as matter and number prior as form, and traces the error to mixing mathematical and definitional standpoints.","ideas":["Units do not differ in quantity or quality","Identifying ideal and mathematical number is the worst view","Limiting number to ten makes the Forms run short","1 is prior as matter, the number prior as form"],"terms":[{"t":"decad","d":"The series up to ten, which some Platonists took as the complete set of ideal numbers."}]},{"i":628,"j":639,"loc":"Book XIII (5 of 5)","title":"Magnitudes, conclusions, and universals versus individuals","summary":"Similar impossibilities arise for lines, planes, and solids generated from species of the great and small or from the point, and for number from the one and plurality. Aristotle concludes that number and magnitudes cannot exist apart, and that the rival versions' discord shows their premises are false. He turns to the believers in Ideas, who make Ideas both universal and separate individuals, tracing this to Socrates' definitions and his successors' separation. A shared difficulty follows: separate substances seem required, but if their elements are individual they are unknowable, and if universal, non-substance is prior to substance. He resolves it partly by distinguishing potential knowledge, which is of the universal, from actual knowledge, which is of a 'this'.","ideas":["Magnitudes cannot be built from great-and-small or points","Disagreement among rivals reflects false hypotheses","Ideas are made both universal and individual","Actual knowledge is of a 'this', potential of the universal"],"terms":[{"t":"katholou (universal)","d":"What is predicated of many; the object of knowledge in its potential sense."}]},{"i":640,"j":646,"loc":"Book XIV (1 of 3)","title":"Contraries, the One as measure, and the great and small","summary":"Book XIV argues that contraries cannot be first principles, since contraries are always predicated of a subject. The Platonists make one contrary matter, the unequal, the great and small, or plurality, set against the One; Aristotle reviews their variants. 'The one' means a measure of a plurality, as a quarter-tone or a foot, so it is not a substance in itself and is not a number. The great and small are relative attributes, not substrata, and the relative is least of all a substance. Finally, eternal things cannot consist of elements, since what has matter can fail to exist.","ideas":["Contraries require a subject and cannot be first principles","The one is a measure, number a measured plurality","The relative is least of all a substance","Eternal substances cannot have elements"],"terms":[{"t":"metron (measure)","d":"The indivisible unit by which a plurality is counted."},{"t":"great and small","d":"The Platonist material principle, here treated as relative attributes."}]},{"i":647,"j":662,"loc":"Book XIV (2 of 3)","title":"Not-being, plurality, and the use of numbers","summary":"Aristotle diagnoses the Platonists' error as answering Parmenides in an obsolete form: they tried to show that not-being is, so that things could be many. But being and not-being have as many senses as the categories, and generation proceeds from the potential, not from falsity. They ask how substances are many but not how qualities or relatives are. He then asks why anyone should believe in numbers, comparing believers in Ideas, those who posit only mathematical number, and the Pythagoreans. Limits are not substances, and on these views nature becomes a series of episodes like a bad tragedy. The elements cannot generate numbers beyond doubling, and generation of eternal numbers is impossible.","ideas":["The Platonists tried to answer Parmenides with not-being","Not-being has as many senses as being","Nature is not a series of episodes","Generating eternal numbers is incoherent"],"terms":[{"t":"to mē on (not-being)","d":"That which is not, in as many senses as being, including the potential."}]},{"i":663,"j":680,"loc":"Book XIV (3 of 3)","title":"The good among principles, and number as cause","summary":"Aristotle asks how the elements relate to the good: some say the good appears only later, as the poets set Zeus over Night and Chaos, while others make the first principle the Best. He holds that the first principle is good, but making the One an element of number has absurd results, making the opposite element the bad-itself. He rejects the claim that the complete comes from the incomplete and asks in what sense number comes from elements. Numbers are not causes as agent, matter, form, or end; essence is a ratio, not a number. Numerological correspondences, such as the seven Pleiades or champions against Thebes, are coincidences, so mathematicals are not separable and not first principles.","ideas":["The first principle is good, but not as an element of number","The bad would be the potentially good","Ratio, not number, is the essence of a mixture","Numerical analogies are coincidences, not causes"],"terms":[{"t":"logos (ratio)","d":"The proportion of components in a mixture, which is its essence rather than a number."}]}]});